/// An underscore may separate digits in a numeric literal.
///
/// 1_000_000 scans today as the integer 1 followed by the identifier _000_000,
/// because '_' begins an identifier.  That is what makes this a scanner change
/// rather than a grammar addition: the two readings overlap and the number
/// scanner has to win.
///
/// ⚠️ The whole rule is that an underscore must have a digit immediately on both
/// sides.  One sentence, and it outlaws every awkward case at once -- leading,
/// trailing, doubled, next to the 0x prefix, next to the point, next to the
/// exponent marker.  Java and Rust permit 1__000; this does not, because a rule
/// that fits in a sentence is worth more than the doubled underscore is.
///
/// Underscores carry no meaning and are removed before the value is read, so
/// 1_000 and 1000 are the same literal and not merely equal ones.
///
/// Fails with: Expect ')' after arguments.  (file rejected -- no test ran)
///
test 'An underscore may separate digits';
begin
    AssertEqual (1000000, 1_000_000);
    AssertEqual (16777215, 0xFF_FF_FF);
    AssertEqual (170, 0b1010_1010);
    AssertEqual (1000.0001, 1_000.000_1);
    AssertEqual (10000000000.0, 1e1_0);
end
